WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.211 --> 00:00:03.000
(emergency alert tone)

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.343
- I only respond when
there's an emergency.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:08.580
- Nope. There you go.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:10.630
Okay. Thanks everybody. I'm just kidding.

00:00:11.886 --> 00:00:13.530
(emergency alert tone)

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.550
- [Reporter] On October 4th,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.200
hundreds of millions of cell phones

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.560
across the US received a text
message and that alert tone

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.130
as part of a test of the
National Emergency Alert System.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.510
- So many of us grew up

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:27.420
with the emergency
broadcast system, right?

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:29.310
- [Narrator] This is a test.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:30.990
This station is conducting a test

00:00:30.990 --> 00:00:33.690
of the emergency broadcasting system.

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.010
This is only a test.

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:38.700
- Getting that tone on
your TV that interrupted

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:41.610
whatever program you were watching.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.400
This is just the modern version of that

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.748
going to people's cell phones.

00:00:46.748 --> 00:00:48.834
- [Reporter] Nationwide
alerts for mobile devices

00:00:48.834 --> 00:00:50.490
were in the works since the

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.550
September 11th, 2001, terrorist attacks,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:56.640
but didn't become operational until 2012.

00:00:56.640 --> 00:00:58.560
Federal law requires the government to

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.950
conduct a nationwide test
at least every three years.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.750
The purpose of the test is to ensure

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.300
that the emergency response
systems continue to

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.780
be effective means of warning
the public about emergencies.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.060
This is the third time
FEMA has conducted a

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:15.690
wireless nationwide test of
all cell phone emergency alerts

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.480
but a second test to all cellular devices.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:20.880
- Getting alerts out
promptly to our communities

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.740
saves lives.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.210
That's why FEMA is working

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:26.310
with the Federal Communications Commission

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.090
and your local public safety
authorities on this test.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:31.200
- [Reporter] There are
four different types

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.450
of wireless emergency alerts;

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:36.840
national or presidential
alerts, which can only be issued

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.540
by the president during
a national emergency,

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.700
imminent threat alerts involving hazards

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:44.100
on the local level, like extreme weather,

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:46.980
AMBER alerts about missing
or abducted children

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.500
and Public safety alerts,
which have information

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.930
about threats that may not be as imminent.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.880
More than 1600 federal, state,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.010
and local authorities use what's known

00:01:56.010 --> 00:01:57.660
as the integrated public alert

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:00.780
and warning system or,
IPAWS, to issue alerts

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.690
and warnings to people
in their jurisdictions.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.190
Most cell phones powered on

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:06.930
within reach of a cell phone tower

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.780
and participating in wireless
service should be triggered

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.250
by the national test.

00:02:11.250 --> 00:02:14.070
Television shows and
radio broadcasts will also

00:02:14.070 --> 00:02:16.950
be interrupted with an
emergency test message.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.710
Only local emergency
alerts like AMBER alerts

00:02:19.710 --> 00:02:23.340
or severe weather warnings
can be disabled in advance.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.410
- You cannot opt out of a national alert

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.050
and that's because it is
sent by the president.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:31.530
So if the president is
deciding to send an alert

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:34.140
it's considered important
enough that you should

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.460
receive it no matter what.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:38.850
Aside from test alerts,
the president has not,

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.210
to my knowledge, ever
sent a national alert.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:43.500
- [Reporter] While the
National Alert System

00:02:43.500 --> 00:02:44.880
has only been tested,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.070
authorities have used
the system locally more

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.150
than 84,000 times since
2012, making it a challenge

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:54.300
for authorities to decide
how often to hit send.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.330
- You know, they're constantly
trying to find that balance

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:01.080
of what's too much and what's too little.

00:03:01.080 --> 00:03:02.670
If you don't send enough alerts,

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.340
then you might not be reaching everyone.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.340
Versus if you send too many,
you might desensitize people.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:13.230
There's very much that balance,

00:03:13.230 --> 00:03:15.900
or that needle that
they're trying to thread.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:18.240
- [Reporter] For FEMA, the
nationwide test is a chance

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.710
to make sure the federal government

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:21.150
can alert the entire country

00:03:21.150 --> 00:03:23.880
in the event of a nationwide emergency.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:26.160
- We want to make sure that when it counts

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.810
we can keep you informed.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.900
The next alert you receive
could save your life

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.883
or the life of ones you love.

00:03:32.883 --> 00:03:35.383
(soft music)

